  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The evaluation relies on synthetic demand generated from static residential address points, which may not fully capture actual dynamic travel behaviors or non-residential trip generators.",
      "what": "The study develops a simulation-based method to evaluate pooled on-demand transit feeder services in areas lacking historical demand data, using resident address points to generate synthetic demand across varying adoption rates. The framework evaluates service viability using three key performance indicators to identify the optimal combination of service area and transit hub. The method was demonstrated by ranking 12 pre-selected feeder zones in Krakow, Poland.",
      "why": "It provides municipal planners with a structured pre-deployment tool to identify viable transit zones and hubs before investing in physical infrastructure or service contracts, removing the requirement for pre-existing travel demand datasets."
    }
